    "title": "What time does Artemis II return to Earth? See splashdown time, NASA schedule",
    "text": "NASA’s Artemis II crew has completed its primary mission objectives and is set to return to Earth in a matter of hours.The Artemis II mission set a new record for the farthest human spaceflight at 6:07 p.m. CT Monday, reaching 252,760 miles from Earth. The crew broke the previous record of 248,655 miles set by Apollo 13 earlier that day.“It is blowing my mind what you can see with the naked eye from the moon right now. It is just unbelievable,” Canadian astronaut Jeremy Hansen radioed. He added that future missions should ensure “this record is not long-lived.”The 10-day mission will conclude Friday with a planned splashdown off the California coast. NASA expects the crew to land in the Pacific Ocean near San Diego at 5:07 p.m. PDT (7:07 p.m. CT) Friday.Here’s what to know about the return schedule.When does Artemis II return to Earth?Artemis II’s lunar flyby and observation period lasted about seven hours, a key milestone in the nearly 10-day test flight. The mission will end with a splashdown Friday, April 10, in the Pacific Ocean.NASA says the spacecraft is following a “free-return” lunar trajectory — a path that uses the gravity of Earth and the moon to guide Orion back while minimizing fuel use.Artemis II return scheduleHere is the planned timeline for April 10 (all times CT):2:05 a.m.: Crew sleep begins10:35 a.m.: Flight Day 10 crew wakeup12:50 p.m.: Orion cabin configuration for re-entry begins1:53 p.m.: Return trajectory correction burn5:30 p.m.: NASA+ coverage begins6:33 p.m.: Crew module and service module separation6:37 p.m.: Crew module raise burn6:53 p.m.: Entry interface7:07 p.m.: Splashdown in the Pacific Ocean9:30 p.m.: Post-splashdown news conference at NASA Johnson Space CenterHow the Navy will help Artemis IIAccording to a Navy press release, the amphibious transport dock USS John P. Murtha (LPD 26) is scheduled to act as the recovery vessel for the Orion spacecraft and its crew when they return from the historic Artemis II mission.The spacecraft is scheduled to splash down off the coast of San Diego at approximately 7:07 p.m. CT (5:07 p.m. PDT). After being recovered by the USS John P. Murtha, the crew will undergo medical evaluations in the ship’s infirmary before heading back to shore and flying to NASA’s Johnson Space Center in Houston.“The U.S. Navy’s amphibious transport dock has unique advantages, including a well deck, helicopter pad, onboard medical facilities, and communication capabilities needed to support the mission,” Petty Officer 1st Class Jomark Almazan said. “The platform gives NASA the ability to recover the Orion space capsule and collect critical data to help ensure it’s ready to recover the astronauts and capsule during future Artemis missions.”",
